# NAME

git-annex transfers - display transfers

# SYNOPSIS

git annex transfers

# DESCRIPTION

A live view of files that are being transferred from and to remotes.

For files that are being received, this often includes a progress meter.
No progress information is available for files that are being sent.

For example:

    receive foo (from darkstar)
    39%   569.1 MiB         2 MiB/s 6m24s
    send bar (to darkstar)

Once a transfer ends, "done" is displayed at the end of its line.
That does not necessarily mean that the transfer finished successfully.

The display of transfers continues until interrupted by eg pressing
control-c.

# OPTIONS

* `--localfilenames`, '-l'

  Usually, the filename displayed by this command is the name provided
  by the initiator of the transfer. So it may not correspond to the name
  of the file in the currently checked out branch. For example, when 
  git-annex-shell is sending a file from a server to a client, the client
  could have a branch checked out that uses a different name for the file.
  Some filenames also need to be mangled for security reasons, or reasons
  of backwards compatability with older versions of git-annex.

  With this option, rather than displaying the filename provided with the
  transfer, display the name of a file in the locally checked out branch
  that corresponds to the git-annex key. If there is no such
  file, the key will be shown instead.

* `--json`

  Enable JSON output. This is intended to be parsed by programs that use
  git-annex. Each line of output is a JSON object.

  Note that display of these objects is deferred until a transfer ends.
  To get currently running transfers, combine this with `--json-progeess`.

* `--json-progress`

  Include progress objects in JSON output.

* Also the [[git-annex-common-options]](1) can be used.

